<title>UK: Austria OMV says signs up for Iraqi crude.</title>
<headline>Austria OMV says signs up for Iraqi crude.</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1996-12-17</dateline>
<text>
<p>Austrian oil company OMV said on Tuesday it had signed a contract with Iraq to lift crude oil under the terms of a United Nations oil-for-food deal.</p>
<p>&quot;It's correct but I can not confirm the volume,&quot; an OMV spokesman told Reuters by telephone from Vienna.</p>
<p>The spokesman said he was &quot;sure&quot; the contract had been approved by the U.N.</p>
<p>Traders familiar with OMV's operations said the company would lift about 15,000 barrels of oil per day.</p>
<p>Iraq exported its first cargo of oil in six years on Monday from the Turkish Mediterranean port of Ceyhan under the deal agreed with the U.N. which permits Baghdad to sell $2 billion worth of its oil over six months on a renewable basis for humanitarian supplies.</p>
<p>The U.N. has also approved crude export contracts for Coastal Corp and Bayoil of the U.S., Turkish state refiner TUPRAS, Dutch trading firm Trafigura, Italian refiner Oil Energy and France's Elf Acquitaine.</p>
<p>--Mark Thompson, London Newsroom +44 171 542 7930</p>
